{"name":"Gem","hex":"#4d5b8a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Gem","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A native or resident of the American state of Idaho."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A ghost town in California.","An unincorporated community in Indiana."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Gem"}],"entries":[{"title":"GEM (desktop environment)","blocks":[{"text":"GEM is a discontinued operating environment released by Digital Research in 1985. GEM is known primarily as the native graphical user interface of the Atari ST series of computers, providing a WIMP desktop. It was also available for IBM PC compatibles and shipped with some models from Amstrad. It was available on the BBC Master computer with an Intel 80186 co-processor. GEM is used as the core for some commercial MS-DOS programs, the most notable being Ventura Publisher. It was ported to other computers that previously lacked graphical interfaces, but never gained traction. The final retail version of GEM was released in 1988."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/IBM_PC_GEM.jpg/330px-IBM_PC_GEM.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GEM_(desktop_environment)","wikidata":"Q849246"},{"title":"Gemstone","blocks":[{"text":"A gemstone is a piece of mineral crystal which, when cut or polished, is used to make jewelry or other adornments. Certain rocks and occasionally organic materials that are not minerals may also be used for jewelry and are therefore often considered to be gemstones as well. Most gemstones are hard, but some softer minerals such as brazilianite may be used in jewelry because of their color, luster, or other physical properties that have aesthetic value. However, generally speaking, soft minerals are not typically used as gemstones by virtue of their brittleness and lack of durability."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Cardinal_gems.png/330px-Cardinal_gems.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gemstone","wikidata":"Q83437"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/CP%E2%81%84M_Ad%2C_InfoWorld%2C_November_29%2C_1982.jpg/330px-CP%E2%81%84M_Ad%2C_InfoWorld%2C_November_29%2C_1982.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/CP%E2%81%84M_Ad%2C_InfoWorld%2C_November_29%2C_1982.jpg/330px-CP%E2%81%84M_Ad%2C_InfoWorld%2C_November_29%2C_1982.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"GSX advertisement in 1982","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACP%E2%81%84M_Ad%2C_InfoWorld%2C_November_29%2C_1982.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Gem_11_Desktop.png/500px-Gem_11_Desktop.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Gem_11_Desktop.png/500px-Gem_11_Desktop.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"GEM 1.1 Desktop running in 640×350 EGA resolution","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGem_11_Desktop.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Gem20about.png/500px-Gem20about.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Gem20about.png/500px-Gem20about.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"GEM 2.0 displaying its Desktop Info dialog. This version of GEM supports 640×480 16-color VGA.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGem20about.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Gem311about.png/500px-Gem311about.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm